Description: open-source image editor with layers, masks and brushes
 PhotoCraft is a native image editor written in Rust, aimed at the kind of work
 Photoshop is used for. It has layers, masks, adjustment layers, layer styles,
 type and brushes, opens and saves layered PSD/PSB files, handles 8, 16 and
 32-bit documents and composites on the GPU (wgpu).
 .
 The headless converter, command runner and MCP server that drive the same
 editor commands are in the storytold-photocraft-cli package.
 .
 PhotoCraft is in early alpha: much of the feature set exists, but upstream
 says it is not yet a replacement for daily professional work.

Package: storytold-photocraft-cli
Architecture: amd64
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends},
         ${misc:Depends},
Suggests: storytold-photocraft (= ${binary:Version}),
         storytold-craft-fonts,
Description: headless command runner and MCP server for the PhotoCraft image editor
 photocraft-cli runs PhotoCraft's editor commands without a window: it converts
 and inspects images, runs the same 500 or so commands as the editor (layers,
 masks, adjustments, painting strokes and so on) and reads or writes layered
 PSD/PSB files.
 .
 photocraft-cli mcp starts an MCP server on standard input and output, so that
 an AI agent can drive the editor engine, headless or attached to a running
 PhotoCraft window, and see the result as an image. It has no windowing or GPU
 dependencies. The storytold-photocraft package provides the editor window.
